WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.350
When it comes to finding a great conflict
to tell a compelling story, you don't get

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.400
much more classic than sibling rivalry.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.980
“ahhh!”

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.940
Cain and Abel were brothers who got in a fight,
and settled their differences with a rock

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:14.440
to the head.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.580
"I guess that settles it."

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.769
So it's no surprise that every movie in the
Thor franchise which depicts Norse gods smashing

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:30.960
monsters and each other with fists, hammers,
and anything else within arm's reach uses

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.420
sibling rivalry as a launching point in all
three installments.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:39.500
The first two feature the conflict and subsequent
reconciliation between brothers Thor and Loki

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.660
as the most important features of their plots.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.740
Meanwhile, Thor: Ragnarok takes things a step
further by introducing a third member of Odin's

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.280
brood, Hela, who takes over Asgard and motivates
her feuding younger brothers to work together

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.200
to defeat her.

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:56.800
But as Marvel Cinematic Universe fans have
since discovered, plans were underway behind

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.120
the scenes to give Thor and Loki yet another,
different sibling a brother who might be more

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.040
familiar to comic book readers and lovers
of Norse mythology.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.380
That's right, Balder the Brave was almost
introduced to the MCU to further complicate

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.240
Thor's family tree.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.900
Former Marvel Studios head of visual development
Charlie Wen sent MCU fans into a frenzy back

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.640
in August 2018 with a single Instagram post.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.120
Wen posted concept art for Thor: The Dark
World that revealed his take on adapting longtime

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.760
Thor comics character Balder to the big screen.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.790
While the character's inclusion in the film
was eventually scuttled, it's exciting to

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:36.040
imagine how the MCU might've been different
had Balder survived pre-production.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.100
So what's the big deal about Balder?

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.880
Well, for starters, he's got more right to
the throne than either Thor or Loki at least

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.760
if Marvel Comics and Norse mythology have
anything to say about it.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.140
The films depict Frigga, played by Rene Russo,
as Thor's mother.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.869
But outside of the films, it's Gaea, the Earth
goddess, who is Thor's real mom, while Frigga

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:56.620
is actually his step-mother.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.340
Wen explained the character's importance during
our exclusive interview:

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.800
"Odin was doing something on the side that
he shouldn't have and then he brought Thor

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.420
baby Thor back and Frigga, and Odin brought
Thor up.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:17.300
In a way it's like Balder is really the true
heir to throne, if you really want to put

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:17.800
it that way.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.380
There's a lot of things about that character
that can be really key to the story."

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.239
However, the filmmakers behind the MCU know
when to stay faithful to the comics, and when

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.920
to streamline and simplify in order to keep
the average moviegoer interested.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:37.000
It's one of the reasons the MCU works as well
as it does and introducing Balder to the mix

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.180
in The Dark World would've made a mess of
an already pretty complicated story one featuring

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:47.100
spacefaring Dark Elves, a reality-warping
Infinity Stone, and Kat Dennings.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.780
Do you even know what these things do?"

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:51.260
"No."

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.160
"Neither do I."

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.240
How could Balder compete with all that?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.780
"It makes a lot of sense not trying to force
him in because he complicates things quite

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.280
a bit.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.720
Especially if you're trying to make a story
really about Thor and Loki."

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.569
But while Wen and the rest of the production
team tried to bring Balder into the fold,

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:14.850
ultimately the biggest hurdle was just that
there was no easy way to introduce the character

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.580
into the MCU's increasingly complex continuity.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.860
At least, there weren't any that also gave
him the importance the comics says he should

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:22.360
have.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:28.300
“In the comics, he is the one that's supposed
to be able to... he triggers Ragnarok.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.780
He's the one that actually triggers that whole
event of the destruction of Asgard.

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:35.580
He's an important character in the comics.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:40.810
How do you integrate him without him feeling
like he's just an added character, right?

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.780
I think that ultimately, not having him in
Thor 1 or 2 makes sense."

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.080
Wen left Marvel Studios to pursue his own
creative endeavors in 2014, so he's not involved

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.240
in the future of the MCU at least not directly.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:00.340
But as it turns out, much of the design work
he did at the company still pops up in Marvel

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.959
movies so many years later.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.340
So, despite the fact that we've yet to see
Balder appear in the MCU to this point doesn't

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.840
mean Wen's designs won't make it onto the
big screen sometime in the future.

